         brute_force_approach='For every single cell, run a full DFS/BFS to see if it can reach the Pacific border AND separately reach the Atlantic border -- correct, but redoes a full traversal from every cell, O((rows*cols)^2) worst case.',
         optimal_approach="Reverse the direction of reasoning: start DFS from every Pacific-touching border cell and every Atlantic-touching border cell simultaneously (two separate visited sets), moving to a neighbor when the neighbor's height is >= the current cell's (the reverse of the actual water-flow direction, but reachability is symmetric this way). A cell in BOTH visited sets is in the answer.",
         common_mistakes="Getting the flow-direction comparison backwards when running from the borders inward -- since you're tracing paths water COULD have taken to reach the border, you move to neighbors whose height is >= (not <=) the current cell; running one full traversal per cell instead of just two traversals total (one seeded from all Pacific-border cells, one from all Atlantic-border cells).",
