         description='Design an algorithm to serialize a binary tree to a string and deserialize that string back to the identical tree structure. This exercise checks the round trip: build a tree, serialize it, deserialize it back, and return the result in level order.',
         constraints='0 <= number of nodes <= 10^4.',
         function_signature='def codec_round_trip(values):',
         starter_code="class TreeNode:\n    def __init__(self, val):\n        self.val = val\n        self.left = None\n        self.right = None\n\ndef build_tree(values):\n    if not values or values[0] is None:\n        return None\n    root = TreeNode(values[0])\n    queue = [root]\n    i = 1\n    while queue and i < len(values):\n        node = queue.pop(0)\n        if i < len(values):\n            lv = values[i]; i += 1\n            if lv is not None:\n                node.left = TreeNode(lv)\n                queue.append(node.left)\n        if i < len(values):\n            rv = values[i]; i += 1\n            if rv is not None:\n                node.right = TreeNode(rv)\n                queue.append(node.right)\n    return root\n\ndef tree_to_level_order(root):\n    if root is None:\n        return []\n    result = []\n    queue = [root]\n    while queue:\n        node = queue.pop(0)\n        if node is None:\n            result.append(None)\n            continue\n        result.append(node.val)\n        queue.append(node.left)\n        queue.append(node.right)\n    while result and result[-1] is None:\n        result.pop()\n    return result\n\n# Implement serialize(node) -> str and deserialize(data) -> TreeNode.\n# A preorder DFS (node, then left, then right) with an explicit marker\n# for 'no node here' is one clean, commonly-used approach.\n\ndef codec_round_trip(values):\n    root = build_tree(values)\n    pass\n",
         expected_time_complexity='O(n) for both serialize and deserialize',
         expected_space_complexity='O(n)',
         brute_force_approach="There's no meaningfully simpler brute force -- any correct scheme must encode enough structural information (including where nodes are MISSING) to reconstruct the exact original shape, not just the set of values.",
         optimal_approach='Preorder DFS serialization: visit each node (value first, then recurse left, then right), using an explicit sentinel (e.g. "N") for None children so the shape is fully recoverable. Deserialization consumes the same tokens in the same preorder sequence, recursively rebuilding each node.',
         common_mistakes="Serializing only the non-None values (e.g. just a level-order list without None markers) -- this loses the tree's actual SHAPE, since multiple different trees can share the same set of values; forgetting that deserialize must consume tokens in EXACTLY the same order serialize produced them in (an iterator over the split tokens, advanced one at a time, is the cleanest way to keep both sides in sync).",
         edge_cases="Empty tree (serializes to just the sentinel, deserializes back to None); single node; a tree that's a straight line (heavily unbalanced) -- shape-preservation matters most here, since a naive scheme is most likely to lose it on lopsided trees.",
         test_inputs=[([1, 2, 3, None, None, 4, 5],), ([],), ([1],), ([1, 2],)],
         reference_solution='class TreeNode:\n    def __init__(self, val):\n        self.val = val\n        self.left = None\n        self.right = None\n\ndef build_tree(values):\n    if not values or values[0] is None:\n        return None\n    root = TreeNode(values[0])\n    queue = [root]\n    i = 1\n    while queue and i < len(values):\n        node = queue.pop(0)\n        if i < len(values):\n            lv = values[i]; i += 1\n            if lv is not None:\n                node.left = TreeNode(lv)\n                queue.append(node.left)\n        if i < len(values):\n            rv = values[i]; i += 1\n            if rv is not None:\n                node.right = TreeNode(rv)\n                queue.append(node.right)\n    return root\n\ndef tree_to_level_order(root):\n    if root is None:\n        return []\n    result = []\n    queue = [root]\n    while queue:\n        node = queue.pop(0)\n        if node is None:\n            result.append(None)\n            continue\n        result.append(node.val)\n        queue.append(node.left)\n        queue.append(node.right)\n    while result and result[-1] is None:\n        result.pop()\n    return result\n\ndef serialize(node):\n    if node is None:\n        return "N"\n    return f"{node.val},{serialize(node.left)},{serialize(node.right)}"\n\ndef deserialize(data):\n    vals = iter(data.split(","))\n\n    def build():\n        v = next(vals)\n        if v == "N":\n            return None\n        node = TreeNode(int(v))\n        node.left = build()\n        node.right = build()\n        return node\n\n    return build()\n\ndef codec_round_trip(values):\n    root = build_tree(values)\n    data = serialize(root)\n    root2 = deserialize(data)\n    return tree_to_level_order(root2)\n',
         hints=["The core challenge is that a plain list of values isn't enough to reconstruct a tree's exact shape -- you need to also encode WHERE the None children are, not just what values exist.", 'A preorder walk (node, then left subtree, then right subtree) with an explicit marker for None is a clean, standard approach. As long as deserialize consumes tokens in that exact same order, it can rebuild the tree unambiguously -- recursion mirrors the serialization structure exactly.', "serialize(node): if None, return 'N'; else return f'{node.val},{serialize(node.left)},{serialize(node.right)}'. deserialize(data): split on commas into an iterator; build() pops one token -- if 'N' return None, else create a node and recursively set node.left = build(), node.right = build()."]
