         brute_force_approach='Recursively try all three operations at every mismatched position without memoization -- exponential, since the same subproblems (prefixes of both strings) get re-explored repeatedly.',
         optimal_approach="This is a direct generalization of Longest Common Subsequence's 2D DP shape: dp[i][j] is the edit distance between word1[:i] and word2[:j]. If the last characters match, dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] (no cost). Otherwise dp[i][j] = 1 + min(dp[i-1][j] [delete], dp[i][j-1] [insert], dp[i-1][j-1] [replace]).",
         common_mistakes="Forgetting the base-case rows/columns (converting an empty string to a string of length k costs exactly k insertions, and vice versa for deletions) -- these seed the first row and column and are easy to accidentally leave at 0; mixing up which of the three neighboring cells corresponds to insert vs delete (the exact assignment doesn't affect the final numeric answer since the recurrence is symmetric in that sense, but it matters if you ever need to reconstruct the actual sequence of operations, not just count them).",
         edge_cases="One string empty (answer is simply the other string's length); identical strings (answer 0); completely disjoint strings with no shared characters at all (answer is close to max(len(word1), len(word2))).",
         test_inputs=[('horse', 'ros'), ('intention', 'execution'), ('', 'abc'), ('abc', 'abc')],
         reference_solution='def min_distance(word1, word2):\n    m, n = len(word1), len(word2)\n    dp = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(m + 1)]\n    for i in range(m + 1):\n        dp[i][0] = i\n    for j in range(n + 1):\n        dp[0][j] = j\n    for i in range(1, m + 1):\n        for j in range(1, n + 1):\n            if word1[i - 1] == word2[j - 1]:\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i - 1][j - 1]\n            else:\n                dp[i][j] = 1 + min(dp[i - 1][j - 1], dp[i - 1][j], dp[i][j - 1])\n    return dp[m][n]\n',
