         brute_force_approach="Simulate every possible ordering of tasks and pick the shortest valid schedule -- combinatorially infeasible. A closed-form math formula also exists (based on the most frequent task's count and how many ties it has) but the heap+queue simulation is more intuitive and generalizes better to related scheduling problems.",
         optimal_approach="At each time step, greedily run whichever task type currently has the MOST remaining instances (tracked in a max-heap, simulated via negated values in Python's min-heap). After running a task, if it still has instances left, it can't be run again until `n` steps later -- hold it in a small cooldown queue of (time it becomes eligible again, remaining count) and push it back onto the heap once that time arrives. If nothing is currently available to run (heap empty but cooldown queue not), the schedule must idle.",
         common_mistakes="Not accounting for idle time when the heap is temporarily empty but tasks are still cooling down -- the total time must still advance even when nothing can run yet; forgetting that Python's heapq is a min-heap, requiring negation to simulate 'run the task with the MOST remaining instances first'.",
         edge_cases='Cooldown of 0 (no waiting needed at all, answer is simply the total task count); a single task type repeated many times with a long cooldown (idle time dominates); enough distinct task types that cooldown is never actually a constraint (answer equals the total task count).',
